{"cuisine/amazonian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/amazonian-cuisine","n":"Amazonian cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Peruvian cuisine","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Peru","intro":[["Amazonian cuisine includes the foods and preparation methods of various peoples in the Amazon jungle of South America, including the dishes they have popularized among neighbors."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"amazonian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazonian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Peru.travel: Amazonian Cuisine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"peru.travel","note":"Section on Peruvian Amazon dishes and ingredients from Peru's official tourism board.","url":"https://www.peru.travel/gastronomy/en/peruvian-cuisine/amazonian-cuisine.html","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Peruvian cuisine","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/lao-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/lao-cuisine","n":"Lao cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Laos","intro":[["Lao cuisine or Laotian cuisine is the national cuisine of Laos."],["The staple food of the Lao is sticky rice. Laos has the highest sticky rice consumption per capita in the world with an average of 171 kilograms (377 lb) of sticky rice consumed annually per person. Sticky rice is deeply ingrained in the culture, religious tradition, and national identity of Laos. It is a common belief within the Lao community that no matter where they are in the world, sticky rice will always be the glue that holds the Lao communities together, connecting them to their culture and to Laos. Affinity for sticky rice is considered the essence of what it means to be Lao. Often the Lao will refer to themselves as luk khao niao (Lao: ລູກເຂົ້າໜຽວ, [lùːk kʰȁw nǐaw]), which can be translated as 'children or descendants of sticky rice'."],["The International Rice Research Institute (IRRI) has described Laos as a \"collector's paradise\". Laos has the highest degrees of biodiversity of sticky rice in the world. As of 2013, approximately 6,530 glutinous rice varieties were collected from five continents (Asia, South America, North America, Europe and Africa) where glutinous rice are grown for preservation at the International Rice Genebank (IRGC). The IRRI gathered more than 13,500 samples and 3,200 varieties of glutinous rice from Laos."],["The trifecta of Laos' national cuisine are sticky rice, laab, and green papaya salad. The most famous Lao dish is laab (Lao: ລາບ, [lâːp]; sometimes also spelled larb, larp or laap), a spicy mixture of marinated meat or fish that is sometimes raw (prepared like ceviche) with a variable combination of herbs, greens, and spices."],["Lao cuisine has many regional variations, corresponding in part to the fresh foods local to each region. A French legacy is still evident in the capital city, Vientiane, where baguettes are sold on the street and French restaurants are common and popular, which were first introduced when Laos was a part of French Indochina."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"lao-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lao_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Saeng's Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"saengskitchen.com","note":"Lao-American cook Saeng Douangdara's recipes and videos of Lao home cooking.","url":"https://www.saengskitchen.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Lao Kitchen: Lao Flavors and Stories Told Through Family Recipes","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Saeng Douangdara","note":"Family recipes from the creator of Saeng's Kitchen.","url":"https://www.penguin.com.au/books/the-lao-kitchen-9780593836170","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780593836170"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":4,"links":[["Drunken noodles","dish/drunken-noodles"],["Khao soi","dish/khao-soi"],["Nam tok (food)","dish/nam-tok-food"],["Thali","dish/thali"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Baby corn","ingredient/baby-corn"],["Bean sprout","ingredient/bean-sprout"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Fish sauce","ingredient/fish-sauce"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Holy basil","ingredient/holy-basil"],["Oyster sauce","ingredient/oyster-sauce"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Pisum sativum subsp. sativum convar. axiphium","ingredient/pisum-sativum-subsp-sativum-convar-axiphium"],["Shahe fen","ingredient/shahe-fen"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tofu","ingredient/tofu"],["Blood as food","ingredient/blood-as-food"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Groat","ingredient/groat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ee/Tray_with_food_in_Laos.jpg/500px-Tray_with_food_in_Laos.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Basile Morin","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tray_with_food_in_Laos.jpg"}},"cuisine/byzantine-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/byzantine-cuisine","n":"Byzantine cuisine","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Historical cuisine","intro":[["Byzantine cuisine was the continuation of local ancient Greek cuisine, ancient Roman cuisine, and Mediterranean cuisine. Byzantine trading with foreigners brought in grains, sugar, livestock, fruits, vegetables, and spices that would otherwise be limited to specific geographical climates."],["Cooks experimented with many new combinations of food, creating two styles in the process. These were the eastern (Asia Minor and the eastern Aegean), consisting of Byzantine cuisine supplemented by trade items, and a leaner style primarily based on local Greek cuisine culture."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"byzantine-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Byzantine_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Flavours of Byzantium","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Andrew Dalby, 2003","note":"Prospect Books history of Byzantine food and drink by classicist Andrew Dalby, with recipes.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781903018149","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781903018149"}]}],"cont":null},"dish/baba-cake":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/baba-cake","n":"Baba (cake)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 4 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["In many European countries, particularly in Central and Eastern Europe, there are various traditions surrounding the use of bread during the Easter holidays. Traditionally the practice of eating Easter bread or sweetened communion bread traces its origin back to Byzantium, Eastern Catholicism and the Orthodox Christian church. The recipe for sweetened or \"honey-leavened\" bread may date back as far as the Homeric Greek period based on anecdotal evidence from classical texts."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-baba-cake"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baba_(cake)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine"],["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":8,"links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Ye